    \"character_backstory\": {
      \"current\": \"Letter from Professor Vael:\
\
Tobias Wren,\
\
I have now read your account three times.\
\
On the first reading, I assumed that some detail had been omitted. On the second, I suspected that your recollection had been distorted by age. On the third, I began to suspect that the inconsistencies themselves may be significant.\
\
Your frustration with them is understandable, but misplaced.\
\
You remember vegetation unlike that surrounding Waterdeep. You remember laughter without a visible source. You remember encountering someone—or something—whose appearance refuses to remain fixed in your memory. Most significantly, your perception of the time spent there does not agree with the time that passed here.\
\
Any one of these observations could be dismissed. Together, they deserve investigation.\
\
Your subsequent aptitude for short-range translocation is harder to dismiss still.\
\
I have considered your theories concerning residual teleportation magic and accidental planar intersection. Neither is impossible. Your suggestion of localized reality distortion is ingenious, although I suspect you are using the phrase chiefly because it permits you to place several things you do not understand into the same category.\
\
Your hypothesis concerning the Feywild is more interesting.\
\
Do not mistake that for confirmation.\
\
There is insufficient evidence.\
\
You have approached this problem as I would expect you to approach it: you are attempting to determine where you went, by what mechanism you arrived there, and what alteration produced the ability you subsequently developed.\
\
Those are reasonable questions.\
\
I am no longer convinced they are the correct ones.\
\
There is one element of your account that you consistently treat as incidental.\
\
You returned.\
\
Whatever occurred, you were a child alone in a place you did not understand, subject to forces you could neither identify nor control. Yet you returned to Waterdeep unharmed, carrying with you an ability you did not possess before.\
\
Perhaps that was chance.\
\
Perhaps you escaped.\
\
Perhaps something sent you back.\
\
Or perhaps you were permitted to leave.\
\
I cannot determine which from the evidence available to me.\
\
Neither can you.\
\
So I suggest that you stop asking, for the moment,  Where you went.\
\
Determine instead: Why you were allowed to return.\
\
I suspect the answer to that question will prove considerably more important.\
\
Professor Elric Vael\
Planar Studies, House of Wonder\
Waterdeep\
\"
    },
